00:27:36.129 --> 00:27:46.080
From NVIDIA Developer: Accelerating Direct Sparse Solvers for Computational Engineering with NVIDIA cuDSS 1.0.

00:27:46.185 --> 00:27:52.324
Sparse linear systems are at the core of nearly every engineering and scientific simulation,

00:27:52.324 --> 00:28:00.205
from structural mechanics and computational fluid dynamics to circuit simulation and power grid analysis.

00:28:00.205 --> 00:28:06.691
As problem sizes and complexity grow, and accuracy needs tighten, developers need a direct

00:28:06.691 --> 00:28:12.607
solver that scales on GPUs without giving up the robustness that production simulations depend

00:28:12.607 --> 00:28:13.968
on.

00:28:14.073 --> 00:28:20.952
From Microsoft Developer Blogs: Do AI coding agents struggle with non-REST APIs?

00:28:21.057 --> 00:28:27.660
Plenty of APIs skip REST conventions. Some send every write over POST, and some

00:28:27.660 --> 00:28:34.771
name every endpoint after an action, like /updateBin or /deleteBin. On paper,

00:28:34.771 --> 00:28:40.146
agents should have a harder time with those. REST maps the intent onto the

00:28:40.146 --> 00:28:44.854
HTTP method, so changing a record is a PATCH and removing it is a

00:28:44.854 --> 00:28:52.118
DELETE. In comparison, an RPC-style API makes the agent work out what each endpoint

00:28:52.118 --> 00:28:57.590
is called and what it does. We expected that to cost agents something, either

00:28:57.590 --> 00:29:03.090
in failed tasks or in extra calls and tokens. It didn’t, at least when

00:29:03.090 --> 00:29:05.568
the agent had the docs.

